ed  quickly  tracking all possible contacts and home  quarantining over 4  300 people they also transferred  negative tested patients to other  hospitals  to decrease the workload on staff after  14 days with no  new confirmed cases all hospital  employees had to do math testing to  convince the public  that the hospital is safe in 44 days the  outbreak was contained  with only 21 cases and just one death  transparency and honesty is the very  important  and you have to give the people the  reason why you want to have  a very stringent concentration and very  stringent home quarantine  and this and the evidence we have to  show the evidence that  it is very very useful to prevent the  future pandemic  chance that no countries can fight alone  i  would like to re-emphasize the important  thing is the prudence surveillance  we have to keep eye on the possible  outbreak in all  the countries to prevent its spread out  now taurian hospital is back to relative  normality  the stars experience reminded the  government and its people  not to let history repeat itself a  lesson not only for taiwan  but for the world in its approach to  tackling this fast-bred environment  jay patel is a researcher on global  covet 19 strategy  at the university of edinburgh and joins  me now from leeds in the uk  jay welcome now you study global  responses  to the coronavirus pandemic in your  assessment  what has taiwan done well  hi well taiwan focused uh very early on  on having an elimination strategy  they were not keen on this idea of  having community transmission where  we've got the virus circulating in a  population  so had a very early very rapid um and  focused effort  to eliminate any cases and clusters that  were emerging  from the initial china connection  and how were they able to implement this  so effectively  did they have any previous experience  with tackling pandemics that came in  handy  well the sars pandemic of 2003  certainly came in handy they had the  planning they had the pre-covered  infrastructure  to deal with pandemics well but above  all they  recognized that acting quickly and  trying to protect public health first of  all in an attempt to  recover economies after the pandemic  were crucial  and certainly applied those lessons to  covet 19 very well  it would appear that what you're saying  is it's quite  it makes a lot of sense to put it simply  i'm just wondering  why other countries could not draw this  same lesson  early on in the pandemic  uh well it's a difficult question to  answer but  perhaps one of the reasons is adopting  an influenza approach of a flu approach  which  many western countries had rather than a  sars plan  i suppose the primary difference between  the two is that flu  favors some level of endemicity i.e some  some circulation in the population to  build immunity  now the problem is that sars coronavirus  2 is much more  lethal it's much more transmissible and  so  that approach just simply would not work  it is also down to the fact that earlier  on in the pandemic  it wasn't really well known how  infectious the new coronavirus  the new coronal virus is  possibly yes um but in  in respect that actually enforces the  need to drive down cases to  as low as possible as quickly as  possible  when there are so many unknowns  exercising what's called the  precautionary principle  certainly has a lot of value and in the  western countries we didn't really see  that approach instead what we saw was a  a firm adherence to the existing flu  plans which were clearly not compatible  with this virus  right we leave it there for the time  being but thank you so much for  joining us jay patel from the university  of edinburgh
 

